\lispPackageChapter{sigma/arrows}

The \texttt{sigma/arrows} package provides threading / arrow macros, including
a Common Lisp port of
\href{https://github.com/rplevy/swiss-arrows}{swiss-arrows} by Robert P.\ Levy
et al.  English names (\texttt{thread-diamond-first},
\texttt{thread-branch-last}, \ldots) are primary; swiss-arrows-style symbols
(\texttt{-<>}, \texttt{-<}, \ldots) are aliases via \texttt{macro-alias},
matching \texttt{sigma/control}'s \texttt{thread-first} / \texttt{->} pattern.
Traditional \texttt{thread-first} (\texttt{->}) and \texttt{thread-last}
(\texttt{->>}) come from \texttt{sigma/control}.

\section{Diamond arrows}

\lispMacroSubsection{thread-diamond-first}

Threads a value through forms, replacing a top-level \texttt{<>} hole when
present; otherwise inserts like \texttt{thread-first}.  Vectors are supported.
Alias: \texttt{-<>} (swiss-arrows diamond wand).

\begin{lstlisting}
(thread-diamond-first 0 (* <> 5) (vector 1 2 <> 3 4))  ; => #(1 2 0 3 4)
(thread-diamond-first 4 (cons '(1 2 3)))               ; => (4 1 2 3)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispMacroSubsection{thread-diamond-last}

Same as \texttt{thread-diamond-first} but default insertion is last
(thread-last style).  Alias: \texttt{-<>>} (swiss-arrows diamond spear).

\begin{lstlisting}
(thread-diamond-last 0 (list 1 2 3))  ; => (1 2 3 0)
\end{lstlisting}

\lispMacroSubsection{thread-some-diamond-first / thread-some-diamond-last}

Nil-shortcutting variants of the diamond macros.  Aliases:
\texttt{some-<>}, \texttt{some-<>>}.

\section{Other arrows}

\lispMacroSubsection{thread-last-reverse}

Like \texttt{thread-last} / \texttt{->>}, but forms are written in reverse
order (last form is the seed).  Alias: \texttt{<<-} (swiss-arrows back arrow).

\lispMacroSubsection{thread-do-first / thread-do-last /
thread-do-diamond-first / thread-do-diamond-last}

Non-updating arrows: run a threading pipeline for side effects, return the
original value.  Aliases: \texttt{-!>}, \texttt{-!>>}, \texttt{-!<>},
\texttt{-!<>>}.

\lispMacroSubsection{thread-apply-first / thread-apply-last}

Applicative arrows: wrap each step in \texttt{apply}.  Aliases:
\texttt{apply->}, \texttt{apply->>}.

\lispMacroSubsection{thread-branch-first / thread-branch-last /
thread-branch-diamond-first / thread-branch-diamond-last}

Branching (furculae): evaluate one expression, then thread it into several
branches with \texttt{thread-first}, \texttt{thread-last},
\texttt{thread-diamond-first}, or \texttt{thread-diamond-last}.  Return a list
of branch results.  Parallel variants:
\texttt{thread-branch-first-parallel}, \texttt{thread-branch-last-parallel},
\texttt{thread-branch-diamond-first-parallel},
\texttt{thread-branch-diamond-last-parallel}.

Symbolic aliases: \texttt{-<}, \texttt{-<<}, \texttt{-<><}, \texttt{-<>><},
and parallels \texttt{-<-p}, \texttt{-<<-p}, \texttt{-<><-p},
\texttt{-<>><-p} (swiss-arrows uses \texttt{-<:p} etc.; Common Lisp avoids
\texttt{:} inside names).  Parallel forms run branches concurrently when
threads are available (SBCL), otherwise sequentially.

\begin{lstlisting}
(thread-branch-first (+ 1 2) (list 2) (list 3) (list 4))
; => ((3 2) (3 3) (3 4))
\end{lstlisting}
